PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: Technically strong set. Every board is unique by line logic and the trap rate is 0 throughout. Each level has a written deduction, so there is no guessing. The 12 chapters x 10 mechanics structure is clean, and the intent text usually matches the board. Casual win rates fall in a believable way, and the last-third average is about 64.5%, which is just inside the ≤65% target. The weaknesses are mostly about feel. (1) Many mid-chapter levels are 'mechanic X plus a mirrored sprite' and feel like filler. (2) Breathers are too often only slightly easier than their neighbours and are not a different kind of experience. (3) Chapters 8-12 stack cracked, tally, smudge, stamp and colour-cross with little relief, so late casual play depends on one trick per level. (4) Grid sizes top out at about 16x14, which is small for a premium nonogram app. (5) A few levels are isolated difficulty spikes: L83, L87, L99 and L111. Vs Picross S and Nonogram.com the set is polished and teachable but short of memorable. Needs fewer filler boards, 2-3 larger showpiece grids, and tighter breathers. Curve: Ramp: L1-12 is a clean teach, practise, boss, breather, twist, combine, finale cycle (full line, exact fit, overlap, split clues, spare cells, voids of width >1). Casual runs 100 down to 82, which is the right slope. Chapter 2 (tally) restarts gently at 90-91% and then ramps to 77% at the boss, which is good. Chapter 3 (colour) opens at 95%, which is a little too easy, and it finishes at 75%. From chapter 4 on, each chapter introduces one new idea (void, stamp, colour-cross, smudge, cracked, colour-tally), which is easy to learn but predictable. The first third is excellent. The middle is serviceable. The last third averages about 64.5% casual, which is on target but only just. Problems: (a) The breathers at L7, L19, L31, L43, L55 and L67 are too close in difficulty to their neighbours. The breathers in chapters 9-12 (L91, L103, L115) are meaningful. L103 sits at 79% next to a 51% boss, which works. (b) L83, L87, L99 and L111 are casual spikes (56-58%) between 70-80% neighbours. (c) Chapters 9-11 repeat the same cracked or tally 'count the rest' deduction too often, so reorder or cut about 4 levels from each. (d) Combine levels L109-L119 re-test mechanics already introduced. A true mixed-mechanic finale should have one 20x20-ish showpiece, but the largest grid is 16x14. (e) The same picture-symmetry template is used repeatedly (L19, L20, L31, L55, L64, L79). Add asymmetric boards in these slots for more variety.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L83 (6/10): Casual 57% is an isolated spike of the 11/12 slot and the pinwheel fold is unreadable by clue logic alone. → Fill the stick column fully and add one more pinwheel vane edge so the order is derivable.
L87 (6/10): Spike: 58% casual among 73-80% neighbours; the cracked run is measured via four columns, which is too heavy for a practise. → Reduce to 9x10 and measure the crack from two columns.
L99 (6/10): Casual 57% / skilled 79% in a practise slot; two interlocking tallies with a guess-like order. → Resolve column 9 first with a stamp or full row, so the ordering is forced.
L111 (6/10): Spike to 56%/85% right after 72%; the crack text reads the same as L112. → Swap the order with L110 and add a full-width anchor row.
L7 (7/10): Row 2's 2 2 'waits for crossings', which is a hesitation point and not a breather. Difficulty is close to L6 and L8. → Add one ink cell to the sun so row 2 resolves right after column 9, or shrink to 9x8 and make every line resolve in sequence.
L15 (7/10): The intent needs two ideas (tally in row 1 and a blank in row 9). Ideas are muddled. → Remove the row 9 tally from the clue set so the level teaches only the row 1 met-count; keep the board.
L19 (7/10): Intent mixes three ideas and the swallow is a near-mirror, so the picture is forgettable. → Use a tally in just one row and give the board an asymmetric tail so the final reveal is a distinct image.
L20 (7/10): A symmetric tree with wide empty margins. Fillable, but the 'aha' is the same as L19, L21. → Offset the trunk and crown so the picture is asymmetric, or cut empty margin columns for a denser 9x12.
L23 (7/10): Fern is a left-leaning blob. The stem is a full column, so it is a free start. The aha is similar to L22. → Break the stem with a gap so column 6 needs a tally cross-check, and make the leaflets uneven.
L31 (7/10): Fine and easy, but the balloon is perfectly symmetric except for one hole, and it repeats the 'full column pair' trick from L25-L29. → Make the balloon lean or move the sandbag so the right rope is not a free copy of the left.
L43 (7/10): It is a repeat of L39-L40 pocket logic, not a rest. Casual 90% is fine, but the board is uninteresting. → Replace with a symmetric dense pagoda with one void only and an obvious full-width eave to start from.
L45 (7/10): Tiny 9x7 and mostly obvious. Void as gap is a nice idea, but it appears once with no follow-up. → Add a second void-gap column so the idea is applied twice, and add one more colour-cross cell.
L49 (7/10): The intent is a single sentence. It teaches 'fixed tile' but not why you cannot deduce it otherwise, and the stamp cell is trivial. → Choose a board where the stamp is the only way to resolve a column that shifts, and show it in the tutorial.
L52 (7/10): Stem stamp is a mild repeat of L49 with colour added; no new insight. → Stamp a colour cell that decides the sage/coral order in a row.
L55 (7/10): Two stamps give a breather feel but mostly mechanical; cat face is generic. → Use a single stamp and a recognisable asymmetric subject with a fully readable brow row.
L57 (7/10): Intent is one line and the stamp is almost an afterthought; board is dense. → Rewrite intent and move stamp to a row whose arc really is ambiguous.
L64 (7/10): Watering can reads oddly and the can body is large and low-information. → Shrink to 10x9 and make the rose the main read; tidy the spout.
L66 (7/10): Casual 68% is on target but the boss opens with a single full column, so it solves like a normal level. → Remove the full trunk column so the opening needs a cross-check.
L67 (7/10): Only 2 passes, so a breather is OK, but the frog repeats the symmetric template. → Offset one eye (wink or tilt) for charm.
L70 (7/10): The midrib is a full column and the leaf is symmetric, so a lot of cells are copied. → Break the symmetry in one half of the leaf slits.
L76 (7/10): The koi is lumpy and the smudge is fully painted from the row above; little new. → Make the smudged row's spots depend on two columns.
L79 (7/10): Candle reads fine but is the third 'smudged row in a pillar' board. → Use a different silhouette, e.g. a bell or drip.
L80 (7/10): Seven passes with three extra for the smudge; adds length, not insight; parasol is cluttered. → Reduce to 11x9 and let one row confirm the smudge.
L86 (7/10): The edge idea is good, but 73% casual is too hard for a teach level and the board is bare. → Add 2 rows of dense fill and make the foot span obvious from one column.
L89 (7/10): Casual 58% for a 5/12 twist when its neighbours are 80%/52% and the chain relies on a 'petals settle' pass. → Give the petals a full-height anchor column.

level-design score 7.4/10 — Technically strong set. Every board is unique by line logic and the trap rate is 0 throughout. Each level has a written deduction, so there is no guessing. The 12 chapters x 10 mechanics structure is clean, and the intent text usually matches the board. Casual win rates fall in a believable way, and the last-third average is about 64.5%, which is just inside the ≤65% target. The weaknesses are mostly about feel. (1) Many mid-chapter levels are 'mechanic X plus a mirrored sprite' and feel like filler. (2) Breathers are too often only slightly easier than their neighbours and are not a different kind of experience. (3) Chapters 8-12 stack cracked, tally, smudge, stamp and colour-cross with little relief, so late casual play depends on one trick per level. (4) Grid sizes top out at about 16x14, which is small for a premium nonogram app. (5) A few levels are isolated difficulty spikes: L83, L87, L99 and L111. Vs Picross S and Nonogram.com the set is polished and teachable but short of memorable. Needs fewer filler boards, 2-3 larger showpiece grids, and tighter breathers.
